  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "The verbs in -er and in -go",
   "texto": [
    "They work almost as in Spanish. **Comer**: like, eat, eat, eat, eat. **Beber**: I drink, you drink, you drink, we drink, you drink.",
    "The **-ir** only changes in «We»: **abrir** → open, open, open, open **i**mos, abrem."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "The reflexive verbs: the pronoun goes behind",
   "texto": [
    "This is one of the great differences with Spanish. In a normal affirmative phrase, the pronoun goes **detrás** of the verb, together with a **guion**: **levanto-me** (I get up), **deito-me** (I lie down), **visto-me** (I get dressed).",
    "But it goes **delante** if there is a denial or a question word: **Não me levanto** cedo. **A que horas te levantas?**"
   ]
  },
